[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Control Flow in C#"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Control flow statements determine the order in which code is executed. C# provides several control flow statements, including 'if/else', 'switch', 'for', 'while', and 'do-while' loops."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"If/Else Statements"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'if' statement executes a block of code if a specified condition is true. The 'else' statement executes a block of code if the same condition is false. You can also use 'else if' to check multiple conditions."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"int x = 10;
if (x > 5)
{
    Console.WriteLine("x is greater than 5");
}
else if (x < 5)
{
    Console.WriteLine("x is less than 5");
}
else
{
    Console.WriteLine("x is equal to 5");
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Switch Statement"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'switch' statement selects one of several code blocks to execute based on the value of an expression. It provides a cleaner way to handle multiple conditional branches compared to nested 'if/else' statements."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"int day = 3;
switch (day)
{
    case 1:
        Console.WriteLine("Monday");
        break;
    case 2:
        Console.WriteLine("Tuesday");
        break;
    case 3:
        Console.WriteLine("Wednesday");
        break;
    default:
        Console.WriteLine("Invalid day");
        break;
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"For Loop"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'for' loop executes a block of code a specified number of times. It consists of an initialization statement, a condition, and an increment/decrement statement."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++)
{
    Console.WriteLine(i);
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"While Loop"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'while' loop executes a block of code as long as a specified condition is true. The condition is checked before each iteration."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"int i = 0;
while (i < 10)
{
    Console.WriteLine(i);
    i++;
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Do-While Loop"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'do-while' loop is similar to the 'while' loop, but it executes the block of code at least once, even if the condition is initially false. The condition is checked after each iteration."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"int i = 0;
do
{
    Console.WriteLine(i);
    i++;
} while (i < 10);"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Break and Continue Statements"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'break' statement terminates the loop or switch statement. The 'continue' statement skips the rest of the current iteration and proceeds to the next iteration."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++)
{
    if (i == 5)
    {
        break; // Exit the loop when i is 5
    }
    if (i % 2 == 0)
    {
        continue; // Skip even numbers
    }
    Console.WriteLine(i);
}"}]
